Looking at this problem, I need to find the minimum number of rectangle-swap moves to transform the grid where cell $(i,j)$ has value $i+j$ into the grid where cell $(i,j)$ has value $2n+2-i-j$.

## Proof

**Key observation.** The target value at position $(i,j)$ is $2n+2-i-j = (n+1-i)+(n+1-j)$, which is exactly the original value at position $(n+1-i,\, n+1-j)$. So the target configuration is the **180° rotation** of the original — equivalently, reversing the columns and reversing the rows.

**Structure of allowed moves.** Since the grid is $n \times n$ and each rectangle must have one dimension equal to $n$, every rectangle is either:
- an $n \times k$ block (spanning all rows, i.e., a block of $k$ consecutive columns), or
- a $k \times n$ block (spanning all columns, i.e., a block of $k$ consecutive rows).

A move swaps two non-overlapping equal blocks of the same type. A column-block swap permutes columns only (leaving rows fixed); a row-block swap permutes rows only (leaving columns fixed).

**Lower bound: at least 2 moves for $n \ge 2$.**

After a single column-block swap, the value at position $(i,j)$ becomes $\sigma(i)+j$ for some column permutation $\sigma$. Setting this equal to the target $2n+2-i-j$ gives $\sigma(i) = 2n+2-i-2j$, which depends on $j$ — impossible for a column permutation.

After a single row-block swap, the value at position $(i,j)$ becomes $i+\tau(j)$ for some row permutation $\tau$. Setting this equal to $2n+2-i-j$ gives $\tau(j) = 2n+2-2i-j$, which depends on $i$ — impossible.

Since every single move is either a column-block swap or a row-block swap, **no single move can achieve the target** for $n \ge 2$. Hence at least 2 moves are required.

**Upper bound: 2 moves suffice.**

*Move 1 — Reverse columns.* Swap the column block $[1,\,\lfloor n/2\rfloor]$ with the column block $[\lceil n/2\rceil+1,\, n]$. These are non-overlapping blocks of equal width $\lfloor n/2\rfloor$ (the middle column, if $n$ is odd, stays fixed). After this swap, column $i$ contains what was in column $n+1-i$, so position $(i,j)$ now holds value $(n+1-i)+j$.

*Move 2 — Reverse rows.* Swap the row block $[1,\,\lfloor n/2\rfloor]$ with the row block $[\lceil n/2\rceil+1,\, n]$, again non-overlapping and of equal height. After this, row $j$ contains what was in row $n+1-j$, so position $(i,j)$ now holds value $(n+1-i)+(n+1-j) = 2n+2-i-j$.

This is exactly the target configuration, achieved in 2 moves.

**Small case.** For $n=1$, the single cell already has value $2 = 2(1)+2-1-1$, so 0 moves are needed.

**Conclusion.** For $n \ge 2$, the minimum number of moves is $\boxed{2}$.

